---
/*
  THE SETS — curated listens (plan 40 §3). Shape locked 2026-08-14 after the
  `/lab/sets` bench: an index of sentences beside ONE open set, and in that set
  a quote, then the description, then the player.

  ⚠ THE THREE ARRANGEMENTS AND THE FOUR QUOTE POSITIONS ARE GONE, and their
  deletion is the point rather than tidying. A bench is legitimate while its
  question is live; `detail` beat `accordion` and `stacked`, `lede` beat `above`
  and `below`, and a shipped component does not carry the losers. What they were
  is in `git log -- src/components/MusicSets.astro`.

  ⚠ WHY `detail` WON, in one move: there is only ever ONE embed and it never
  moves. From that follows everything —

    1. The player can be TALL. Nine tracks instead of two. Seven embeds deep
       enough to be useful would be several thousand pixels of somebody else's
       green, so "taller" and "all at once" could never both be true.
    2. Michael's sentence is printed ONCE, in his type. The index carries the
       title, so this pane renders NO HEADING — and Spotify's copy of the same
       sentence stays over inside the embed instead of six pixels under ours.
       `stacked` could not dodge that and it is what killed it.
    3. Seven sentences in display type become the page, which is the strongest
       thing on it.
    4. The frame is stationary, so `scripts/music-sets.ts` swaps sets with
       `loadEntity` rather than tearing down an iframe. `accordion` moves the
       frame between list items and re-parenting an iframe reloads it — that was
       structural, not a missing optimisation.

  ⚠ AND THE QUOTE LEADS. It sits in the hole the missing heading leaves, so the
  pane opens in someone else's voice while the index answers it in Michael's
  from the left column: two voices, one per column. Below it his description,
  and the player last — which is `ConstellationSuite` inverted rather than a new
  idea. That page runs description → SCORE, small → the suite, long; the score
  accompanies and the work goes last. Here the quote accompanies and the
  playlist is the work.

  ⚠ NO FEELING WORDS. Filtering needs volume, and a title like "What would you
  still do, if you knew you would fail?" IS redemptive, inspiring and defiant —
  printing those three words under it would explain the joke, the same call
  `MusicRoom` made when it put a song's feelings below the player rather than
  above. The vocabulary retires with the room (plan 40).
*/
import StarMark from './StarMark.astro';
import { embedForUrl } from '../lib/media';
import { renderMarkdown, toPlainText } from '../lib/markdown';
import { quoteSize } from '../lib/quote-scale';
import { setHref, type MusicSet } from '../lib/music-sets';

/*
  ⚠ BELOW `md` THE INDEX IS THE PAGE AND A SET OPENS OVER IT — ruled 2026-09-01
  on `/lab/sets`, and the side-by-side layout above `md` is untouched. Michael:
  *"on mobile I don't like how on the listening page we just stack the list of
  playlists on top of the viewing section. I think this makes sense in the
  desktop viewer, where we can view it side by side, but just stacking it is not
  a good UI/UX solution."*

  WHAT STACKING ACTUALLY COST, measured on a 390×844 phone: the index of eight
  titles is **471px tall and starts at y=92**, so it IS the first screen; the
  detail pane started at y=611 and the player at **y=783**, below the fold before
  browser chrome takes another hundred pixels. Two failures, and the second is
  the worse one:

    1. A stranger arriving cold saw eight sentences and no evidence the page had
       playlists on it at all — and this room prints no heading, by deliberation,
       because on desktop the player beside the index is the explanation. Below
       `md` there was no player beside anything.
    2. Tapping a title ran the in-place swap ENTIRELY OFF-SCREEN. `aria-live` had
       been announcing it to screen readers the whole time, which is the tell:
       the pane knew it had changed and only sighted readers were not told.

  ⚠ THE SHEET IS `position: fixed`, NOT A `<dialog>`, AND THAT IS THE ROOM'S
  OLDEST CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. Moving the pane into the top layer
  means moving the NODE, and re-parenting an iframe reloads it — the same fact
  that killed `accordion` and `stacked` on the first bench. So the pane stays
  exactly where the server put it and only its box changes. The site owns a
  perfectly good sheet in `Reader.astro` and this one cannot use it.

  ⚠ AND DISMISSING IT DOES NOT STOP THE MUSIC. The pane is still in the DOM, so a
  playing embed keeps playing behind the index with no visible control. That is a
  KNOWN AND ACCEPTED cost, judged on the bench before the ruling rather than
  discovered after it: the alternative is a persistent mini-bar, and a room whose
  entire product is *"press Save inside a player once and never come back"* should
  not grow a playback UI to manage a session it does not want you to have. ADR
  0032's question — what does dismissing cost — is answered "nothing you typed",
  which is the whole of what that record governs.

  ⚠ THREE RIVALS ARE DELETED RATHER THAN KEPT AS OPTIONS: `stack` (the control,
  what shipped), `lead` (the open set first, index beneath), `scroll` (today's
  order, tap glides the pane into view). A bench is legitimate while its question
  is live; `git log -- src/components/MusicSets.astro` has all three.
*/

/*
  ⚠ AN INDEX SENTENCE SAYS IT IS A DOOR WITH HUE AND A STAR — ruled 2026-09-02 on
  `/lab/afford-sets`. Michael: *"can we style the listening sets so its more
  obvious theyre clickable? similar to constellations?"* and then, on the mark:
  *"just a star to the left of each one thats kind of faded and then glows when
  hovered or active."*

  WHAT WAS WRONG, measured rather than felt. A constellation name rested at
  `oklab(0.859 0.017 0.048)` — a TRACE OF ITS OWN HUE, at full opacity. A set
  rested at `oklab(0.91 0.004 0.018 / 0.57)`: no hue at all, and 57% alpha (69%
  on paper). Faded text is the universal convention for DISABLED, so the seven
  sets a reader could open were styled like dead items while the one already open
  wore the only colour on the page. **The grammar was backwards — hue marked the
  place you were rather than the places you could go.**

  ⚠ AND THE ONLY AFFORDANCE WAS A `:hover` RULE, which is the whole finding of
  ADR 0022 — *"hover is not a weaker signal on touch, it is an absent one"* —
  written after Michael reported exactly this about the Sky a month earlier.
  `QuoteCard` learned it (it rests at `color-mix(base-content 96%, primary)` and
  cites `.sky-name` by name); this component was written a week later and adopted
  neither the trace nor `focusTracker`. It was the last public index on the site
  affording by hover alone, and since the sheet shipped this index IS the whole
  first screen below `md` rather than a column beside a player.

  ⚠ THE SETS INDEX HAS ONE MORE STATE THAN THE SKY DOES, which is why this is an
  adaptation rather than a port. An overview row is a door or it is focused — two
  states. A set is a door, the door you are about to take, or **the door you
  took**. Today focus and open share one treatment (full hue, lit star), which is
  deliberate but not obviously right: it means a hovered set and the open set look
  identical. `open` had four rivals on the bench for freeing hue — `rule`,
  `flush`, `weight`, `none` — and none was chosen. `git log` has them if the
  collision starts to read as ambiguity.

  ⚠ AND `flush` CANNOT BE REVIVED AS IT WAS. It marked the open set by removing
  its indent, and the lean it spent is gone (see `lib/music-sets.ts`) — it also
  could not mark three of the eight sets, whose indents were already zero.
*/

interface Props {
  sets: MusicSet[];
  /** Always a real slug — `resolveSet` guarantees it. */
  selected: string;
  /**
   * ⚠ THE EMBED HEIGHT IS A FREE PARAMETER AND 352 IS A CONVENTION, NOT A
   * CEILING. Spotify's own docs pass `height: '200'` and `'160'`; the player
   * fills whatever it is given with more track rows. Michael, 2026-08-14: *"it
   * only shows 2 songs at a time in what little space it has"*.
   *
   * ⚠ 480 IS RULED FOR THE SHEET (2026-09-01) — Michael, deciding it alongside
   * the arrangement: *"480 embed height for mobile is ideal as well."* It was
   * already the default here, so nothing changed; what changed is that it is now
   * a decision rather than a placeholder. 600 leaves ~250px of dead space under
   * a three-track playlist and 480 does not.
   *
   * The parameter stays, and `/lab/sets` keeps its switch, because the number
   * that suits a full-screen sheet is not necessarily the number that suits a
   * 20rem column beside an index — and that half has not been looked at with
   * sets this long.
   */
  height?: number;
  base?: string;
}
const { sets, selected, height = 480, base = '/listening' } = Astro.props;

const open = sets.find((s) => s.slug === selected) ?? sets[0];
const openEmbed = open ? embedForUrl(open.url) : null;
const descOf = (s: MusicSet) => (s.description.trim() ? renderMarkdown(s.description) : '');

/* ⚠ COMPUTED PER SET, NOT PER RENDER, because this pane swaps its quote in the
   browser without a navigation — so the size cannot be a property of the OPEN
   set, it has to travel with every set in the payload at the foot of this file.
   `music-sets.ts` applies the two numbers on the swap and never learns the
   curve, which is what `quoteStyle`'s note means about the one client that
   rewrites a quote.

   ⚠ MEASURED ON `toPlainText`, matching the other three surfaces, even though
   this one prints the body as plain text rather than rendering it. The ramp is
   about how much line there is to LOOK at, and a `**` is two characters nobody
   sees. (That this surface prints the mark at all is a separate latent bug —
   `fragments.body` is Markdown by contract and the other three honour it.) */
const quoteSizeOf = (s: MusicSet) => (s.quote ? quoteSize(toPlainText(s.quote.text).length, 'listening') : null);
const openQuoteSize = open ? quoteSizeOf(open) : null;

/* ⚠ THE COLOUR LEFT THIS STRING ON 2026-09-02 and moved into the stylesheet
   below. It was `isOpen ? 'text-primary' : 'text-whisper hover:text-base-content/75'`
   — inherited from the deleted room's grammar for a chosen word
   (`.feel-word.is-on`), which is a SELECTION grammar that was being asked to
   carry AFFORDANCE as well and could not. See the header.

   ⚠ `.set-line` IS ALSO THE FOCUS ELEMENT, the way `.sky-row` is on the
   overview — `focusTracker` writes `.is-focus` onto these anchors, so a thumb
   gets what a cursor gets. Keep the two class names in step with the CSS.

   ⚠ NO `block` AND NO `transition-colors`: the stylesheet makes this a flex
   baseline row for the star, and transitions colour at the same 0.4s as the
   mark's glow so the two arrive together. Tailwind's 300ms would desynchronise
   them by a tenth of a second, which is exactly the *"checklist of effects"*
   ADR 0022 built its three signals to avoid. */
const INDEX_ITEM = 'set-line font-display text-xl leading-snug font-light sm:text-2xl';

/* Italic against the description's upright, because both are serif at a similar
   size and without a slant they read as one block of small text. Italic for a
   borrowed voice is also what the constellation epigraph already does. */
/* ⚠ THE SIZE LEFT THIS STRING ON 2026-09-01 and moved onto the element as two
   custom properties (`lib/quote-scale.ts`). It was `text-xl sm:text-2xl` — flat,
   from the corpus's shortest line to its longest — and this was the fourth of
   four surfaces that set a quote and the third of them that never ramped.
   1.25/1.5rem is still the ceiling, so a short epigraph is unchanged. */
const QUOTE_TEXT = 'quote-ramp font-serif leading-relaxed italic text-muted';
const QUOTE_CITE = 'font-serif text-[0.9rem] tracking-[0.08em] [font-variant:all-small-caps] text-whisper';
---

{
  sets.length > 0 && (
    <Fragment>
      {/* ⚠ TWO ORIGINS, AND THE SECOND MATTERS MORE. `open.spotify.com` serves
          the embed document and its bundle; `i.scdn.co` serves the cover art,
          which is a separate origin and is what the eye waits for on a switch.
          In the body rather than the head because this is a component and the
          alternative is threading a head slot through the layout for two links;
          browsers honour `preconnect` wherever it appears. */}
      <link rel="preconnect" href="https://open.spotify.com" />
      <link rel="preconnect" href="https://i.scdn.co" crossorigin />

      {/* `.sets-room` is what the sheet's CSS and `music-sets.ts` both hang off —
          the open state is one class on this element and nothing else moves. */}
      <section
        class="sets-room mx-auto grid w-full max-w-6xl grid-cols-1 gap-12 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] md:gap-16"
        aria-label="Playlists"
      >
        <nav aria-label="Sets">
          <ul class="flex flex-col gap-7">
            {sets.map((s) => {
              /* ⚠ A COLD LOAD STILL MARKS A SET NOBODY CHOSE, and that is
                 unchanged rather than endorsed. `resolveSet` falls back to the
                 FIRST set when the URL names none, so a stranger's first screen
                 has one lit star and one full-hue sentence they never picked —
                 and below `md` it is a lie about state besides, since the sheet
                 is shut and nothing is open. Benched as `cold=off` and not
                 ruled on; it costs much less now that the other seven read as
                 doors rather than as dead items, which is why it was left. */
              const isOpen = s.slug === open?.slug;
              return (
                <li>
                  <a
                    href={setHref(s.slug, base)}
                    data-set={s.slug}
                    aria-current={isOpen ? 'true' : undefined}
                    class:list={[INDEX_ITEM, isOpen && 'is-open']}
                  >
                    {/* ⚠ `StarMark`, NOT `StarBloom`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A
                        CLAIM. The bloom's own header: *"a constellation is drawn
                        BETWEEN stars, never one star"* — it parts to say
                        "several things live here". A set is one playlist, so
                        blooming it would borrow a sentence about constellations
                        and say it about a Spotify link. A single point is the
                        honest mark. (If the reading is meant to be "many songs
                        live here", the bloom is right and this is a one-word
                        change — worth asking before it ships.) */}
                    <span class="set-star" aria-hidden="true">
                      <StarMark />
                    </span>
                    {/* Wrapped so the flex row has a text box to wrap INSIDE:
                        without it a two-line title's second line would return to
                        the star's left edge instead of hanging under the first. */}
                    <span>{s.title}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              );
            })}
          </ul>
        </nav>

        {open && (
          /* ⚠ NO HEADING IN HERE, ON PURPOSE — see the file header. The selected
             sentence in the index is this pane's title, which is the whole
             reason the double print goes away. `aria-live="polite"` because
             pressing an index item replaces this region without moving focus,
             and a screen reader otherwise gets no signal that anything
             happened. */
          <div id="set-detail" aria-live="polite" data-height={height} data-base={base} data-open={open.slug}>
            {/* ⚠ THE SHEET'S ONLY CHROME, AND IT IS A BACK ARROW RATHER THAN AN
                ✕ ON PURPOSE — `Reader.astro` settled that in the same words: the
                intent is "leave this", not "close a panel", because there is a
                whole page behind it to go back TO. Same reason it prefers
                `history.back()`; see the script.

                First in the markup because it is what a thumb should reach
                first. Renders only below `md`, where a sheet exists to leave. */}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="sets-back text-whisper font-sans text-xs tracking-wide uppercase"
              data-set-close
            >
              ← Back to the sets
            </button>

            {/* ⚠ THE PANE'S OWN TITLE, AND IT IS NOT THE DOUBLE PRINT THE FIRST
                BENCH KILLED. That one was Michael's sentence and then Spotify's
                copy of it six pixels below — two voices saying the same thing at
                once, which is what killed `stacked`. This is the SAME single
                print relocated: once the set is a sheet, the index that was
                serving as this pane's heading is behind it, and an untitled
                sheet opening on a borrowed quote reads as nobody's.

                Above `md` it does not render at all, because there the index is
                still overhead and still doing the job. `aria-hidden` because the
                index entry it repeats is already the accessible name of what is
                open — see `aria-current` above. */}
            <p class="sets-lede font-display text-primary mb-7 text-2xl leading-snug font-light" aria-hidden="true">
              <span data-set-title>{open.title}</span>
            </p>

            {/* Always rendered even when empty — the client writes the next
                set's quote into it, and a container that appears and disappears
                is a layout shift on every switch. */}
            <figure id="set-quote" class="prose-measure" hidden={!open.quote}>
              <blockquote
                class={QUOTE_TEXT}
                style={openQuoteSize ? `--qs:${openQuoteSize.sm}rem;--ql:${openQuoteSize.lg}rem` : undefined}
              >
                {/* A quote may carry the line break its author put there. */}
                <p class="whitespace-pre-line" data-quote-text>
                  {open.quote?.text}
                </p>
              </blockquote>
              <figcaption class:list={['mt-2', QUOTE_CITE]}>
                <span data-quote-author>{open.quote?.author}</span>
                {/* ⚠ ITALIC, NOT FAINTER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. The work
                    used to sit at 20% ink so it would read as subordinate to the
                    author — 1.5:1 on dusk, unreadable. The citation as a whole is
                    already on `--text-whisper`, which is the floor, so there is no
                    transparency left to spend. Italic does the same subordinating
                    job, and it is what a work title wants anyway. Keep this in
                    step with `scripts/music-sets.ts`, which builds the same span
                    when a quote arrives client-side. */}
                {open.quote?.work && (
                  <span class="italic" data-quote-work>
                    , {open.quote.work}
                  </span>
                )}
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <div
              id="set-desc"
              class="cn-desc prose-measure text-faint mt-7 font-serif text-lg empty:mt-0"
              set:html={descOf(open)}
            />

            {/* ⚠ THE CLIP LIVES ON THIS WRAPPER, NOT ON THE IFRAME, because we
                do not own the iframe: `createController` replaces the mount with
                its own element and gives it `border-radius: 0`. Spotify paints a
                12px-rounded card inside a frame whose document canvas is WHITE,
                and with nothing clipping it that canvas shows at all four
                corners. Measured at 4× on 2026-08-14 — it is the embed's own
                background, not something showing through from behind. */}
            <div
              id="set-embed-slot"
              class="relative mt-8 overflow-hidden"
              style={`height:${height}px;border-radius:var(--radius-box)`}
            >
              {/* ⚠ THE SKELETON SITS BEHIND THE PLAYER RATHER THAN INSTEAD OF
                  IT. The frame above starts at `opacity:0` and is revealed on
                  the embed's own `ready` event — which, measured 2026-08-14,
                  RE-FIRES on every `loadEntity` about 270ms after the call
                  rather than only at init. So nobody sees a half-built player:
                  they see this box, then the finished thing fading in over it.

                  Deliberately not a spinner. This site builds distinctiveness
                  from structure and type rather than iconography, and a spinner
                  would be the only piece of UI-kit chrome on a public page. */}
              <div class="set-skeleton absolute inset-0" style="border-radius:var(--radius-box)" />

              <div id="set-embed-frame" class="relative h-full opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-500 ease-out">
                {/* A PLACEHOLDER, NOT AN IFRAME. `createController` REPLACES the
                    element it is given, so server-rendering a real frame and
                    then building a controller over it costs two document loads.
                    The box is reserved at the final height so the text above it
                    never moves. */}
                <div id="set-embed-mount" class="w-full" style={`height:${height}px`} />
              </div>

              {openEmbed && (
                <noscript>
                  <iframe
                    src={openEmbed.src}
                    width="100%"
                    height={height}
                    loading="lazy"
                    allow={openEmbed.allow}
                    title={open.title}
                    style="border-radius: var(--radius-box); border: 0"
                  />
                </noscript>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>

      {/* Every set's quote and prose, inert, so the client can change panes
          without a fetch. Cheap — eight short records — and it is what lets a
          switch cost no navigation.

          ⚠ THE QUOTE CARRIES ITS OWN TWO SIZES, and that is the whole reason the
          browser never needs `quote-scale.ts`. A quote's size is a function of
          its length, and this pane is the one surface on the site that replaces
          a quote WITHOUT a navigation — so either the curve ships to the client
          or the answers do. The answers are two numbers per set. */}
      <script
        type="application/json"
        id="set-data"
        is:inline
        set:html={JSON.stringify(
          Object.fromEntries(
            sets.map((s) => [
              s.slug,
              {
                title: s.title,
                desc: descOf(s),
                url: s.url,
                quote: s.quote ? { ...s.quote, size: quoteSizeOf(s) } : null,
              },
            ]),
          ),
        )}
      />
    </Fragment>
  )
}

<style>
  /* ── how an index sentence says it is a door ────────────────────────────────
     Ruled 2026-09-02 on `/lab/afford-sets`. The header carries the diagnosis and
     the rivals; what survives is a trace of hue at rest, a star in the gutter,
     and full colour plus a glow on whichever sentence is being attended to.

     ⚠ THE TWO SIGNALS ARRIVE TOGETHER ON PURPOSE, which is ADR 0022's rule in
     its own words — three signals *"deliberately on the same focus and the same
     easing so the row reads as one arrival rather than a checklist of effects."*
     Same 0.4s, same trigger, no delay between them. */

  .set-line {
    /* A flex baseline row, the way the overview pairs its star with its name.
       `baseline` rather than `center`: the mark sits on the sentence's baseline
       instead of floating in the middle of a two-line block. The title is
       wrapped in its own span so a second line hangs under the first rather than
       returning to the star's left edge. */
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.55em;
    /* ⚠ HUE AT FULL OPACITY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FIX. This rested at
       `--text-whisper` — 57% alpha on dusk, 69% on paper, no hue at all — which
       is the disabled convention, so the seven sets a reader could open were
       styled like dead items while the one already open wore the only colour on
       the page. `.sky-name` does the opposite and says so: a TRACE of the lamp
       mixed into the prose ink, because *"a stranger who lands and never moves
       still sees that these are links"*.

       22% of `--color-primary`. The site carries this device at 34% of `--cn`
       on the Sky and 4% of primary in the feed; 38% here measured
       `oklab(0.853 0.019 0.051)` against `.sky-name`'s
       `oklab(0.859 0.017 0.048)` — near enough to be the same signal — and this
       index sits one step quieter than the front door deliberately. */
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-primary) 22%, var(--color-base-content));
    /* NOT `transition-colors` from the class list: the star transitions too, and
       both are declared at 0.4s so they arrive as one thing. */
    transition: color 0.4s ease;
  }

  /* ⚠ `.is-focus` IS WRITTEN BY `focusTracker`, NOT BY `:hover`, and that is the
     point — ADR 0022: *"hover is not a weaker signal on touch, it is an absent
     one."* On a phone this lands on the sentence nearest the reading line; on a
     pointer device, under the cursor. One rule serves both because the script
     already decided which input to consult. `.is-open` joins it because the set
     you took should read as attended to for as long as it is open. */
  .set-line.is-focus,
  .set-line:focus-visible,
  .set-line.is-open {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  /* ── the mark ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ⚠ `StarMark`, NOT `StarBloom`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A CLAIM — see the note
     at the call site. A single point, because a set is one playlist. */
  .set-star {
    /* `em` off the sentence, so one rule holds at both index sizes — the same
       reason `StarMark` is em-sized at all. */
    font-size: 0.62em;
    flex: none;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    /* "Kind of faded" — far enough down that the glow has somewhere to travel,
       high enough that the column reads as eight marks rather than as dust. */
    opacity: 0.38;
    transition:
      opacity 0.4s ease,
      color 0.4s ease;
  }

  /* ⚠ THE GLOW IS ON THE MARK, NOT THE SPAN. `.sky-star`'s reasoning, borrowed
     rather than its code: *"a filter rasterises its whole subtree and a
     descendant cannot opt out"*, so anything ever added inside this span would
     silently be lit too.

     ⚠ AND IT IS DECLARED AT REST WITH A TRANSPARENT COLOUR so only ALPHA moves.
     Without it the filter interpolates from `none`, which is the identity, and
     the BLUR RADIUS grows with the colour — a halo popping in a beat late
     rather than a light coming up. Measured once already, on `.sky-cta__arrow`,
     where it cost a bug.

     ⚠ `:global()` IS LOAD-BEARING AND ITS ABSENCE FAILS SILENTLY. `.star-mark`
     is rendered by `StarMark.astro`, so it carries THAT component's scope id;
     Astro compiles a bare `.set-star .star-mark` into a selector demanding this
     file's `data-astro-cid-*` on both halves, which the svg will never have. No
     error, no warning — the rule matches nothing and every mark measures
     `filter: none`. (`admin.css`'s `.sky-star .star-mark` needs none of this
     because a plain stylesheet is not scoped at all.) */
  .set-star :global(.star-mark) {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 7px transparent);
    transition: filter 0.4s ease;
  }
  .set-line.is-focus .set-star,
  .set-line:focus-visible .set-star,
  .set-line.is-open .set-star {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  .set-line.is-focus :global(.star-mark),
  .set-line:focus-visible :global(.star-mark),
  .set-line.is-open :global(.star-mark) {
